At 30 September 2025, Palm Hills current assets of EGP99,982,419 thousand minus current liabilities of 98,786,344 thousand equal positive net current assets of 1,196,075 thousand. Page 2's reported net debt is rounded EGP4.2 billion. Net current assets are an accounting residual, not spendable cash; the narrow positive difference does not prove either solvency or insolvency.

Real-estate current assets may include development inventories and long collection schedules. Current liabilities may include customer advances whose economic settlement requires delivering units, not merely repaying financial debt. Missing composition prevents treating every liability as equivalent borrowing. Page 4's cash of 9,204,446 thousand and positive operating cash flow are supportive, but cash restrictions, pledged funds, debt maturities and committed construction/land payments are absent. The 248 billion receivables figure expressly includes off-balance-sheet amounts and must not be added to balance-sheet assets or assumed immediately collectible. Net-debt definitions, off-balance obligations, guarantees and recoverability haircuts need reconciliation. I would wait for full footnotes and a dated sources-and-uses schedule; neither cash runway nor liquidation value can be calculated credibly from these selected totals. The favorable net-current-assets sign merits follow-up, not unconditional investment clearance.
